util/qemu-timer: do not round sub-millisecond main-loop waits up on Darwin

Darwin has no ppoll, so qemu_poll_ns falls back to g_poll, whose timeout is in
milliseconds: every sub-millisecond deadline is rounded UP to a full 1 ms, and a
device timer armed 5 us out can fire 200x late. Poll with a zero timeout for
those instead. The spin is bounded by the deadline itself, which is by
definition shorter than one poll tick.

diff --git a/util/qemu-timer.c b/util/qemu-timer.c
index d93a020064..d9c23dbac5 100644
--- a/util/qemu-timer.c
+++ b/util/qemu-timer.c
@@ -343,6 +343,16 @@ int qemu_poll_ns(GPollFD *fds, guint nfds, int64_t timeout)
         return ppoll((struct pollfd *)fds, nfds, &ts, NULL);
     }
 #else
+    /*
+     * No ppoll (Darwin): g_poll's timeout is milliseconds, so every
+     * sub-millisecond deadline is rounded UP to a full 1 ms and a device
+     * timer armed 5 us out fires up to 200x late. Spin the main loop for
+     * those instead — the wait is by definition shorter than one poll tick,
+     * so the CPU cost is bounded by the deadline itself.
+     */
+    if (timeout > 0 && timeout < SCALE_MS) {
+        return g_poll(fds, nfds, 0);
+    }
     return g_poll(fds, nfds, qemu_timeout_ns_to_ms(timeout));
 #endif
 }
